Abstract

Nowadays smartphone distractions are frequently encountered while driving and are causing an increasing number of crashes. A method to prevent them is related to the use of advanced driver assistance systems (ADAS) which ensures that the driver's attention will return to the road when he/she is tempted to use a smartphone. An experimental study based on a driving simulator was conducted in order to investigate the influence of an in-vehicle smartphone based ADAS on the drivers' behavior when they are distracted by social networking applications. A number of 17 participants performed three scenarios consisting in driving with and without distraction factors related to various Facebook tasks with and without ADAS notifications. The statistical analysis shows that the use of the ADAS application help in reducing driving infractions on average by 43.43% compared to the situation in which the driver does not use it.
Introduction

Over the last few years smartphones involuntarily became part of our daily lives, accompanying us almost everywhere we go. Thus, besides the traditional functionality of mobile phones by means of these devices we can access a wide range of activities such as: browsing the web, emailing, social networking, GPS navigation, browsing and playing personal media or playing different online or offline games (Basacik, Reed, & Robbins, 2011). According to the latest annual Ericsson Mobility Report the worldwide smartphones subscriptions increased, exceeding the number of subscriptions for mobile phones, the report indicating that 55 percent of all subscriptions are for smartphones. Also, in the report is shown that smartphones accounted for almost 80 percent of all sold mobile phones (Ericsson, 2016).

In the past, people were involved in activities like eating food or drinking non-alcoholic beverages while driving. Today, due to the increased number of smartphone users and also due to the expansion and improvement of telecommunication services, new types of driving distractions are starting to appear (Drews, Yazdani, Godfrey, Cooper, & Strayer, 2009). As a result, new potential health risks are developed for drivers who engage in these distraction activities but also for vehicle passengers, pedestrians or other drivers (Kass, Cole, & Stanny, 2007).

An increased number of drivers reported having used smartphones in their vehicles to access the Internet, to read or respond to e-mails, to read or update social media networks, resulting in fact that nearly one of four drivers are going online while driving. Moreover, the use of mobile phones while driving is not just a problem for young people, significant percentages being reported among drivers over 40 years (USA Today, 2013).

Many research studies on distracted driving caused by using mobile phones have focused on texting and reading (Bayer and Campbell, 2012, Caird et al., 2014, Cook and Jones, 2011, Cooper et al., 2011, Hosking et al., 2009, Nemme and White, 2010, Olsen et al., 2013, Rumschlag et al., 2015, Yannis et al., 2014, Young et al., 2014). In all cases the researchers found that using this type of distractions can significantly diminish the driving performance and create a crash risk 23 times greater than when not doing so (Olson, Hanowski, Hickman, & Bocanegra, 2009). Moreover, according to the Virginia Tech Transportation Institute a driver takes off his eyes from the road for an average of 4.6 s while reading or sending a text message (Virginia Tech Transportation Institute, 2009). Another research conducted in a car simulator revealed that the participants’ reaction time was reduced by approximately 38% while accessing a social network on their smartphones. The researchers think that this kind of distraction is more dangerous than other driving behaviors (Institute of Advanced Motorists, 2012).

To prevent unsafe/dangerous situations from happening, over the past few years advanced driver assistance systems (ADAS) have been developed. These technologies provide assistance during the driving process (e.g. anti-lock braking system - ABS), monitor what happens around the vehicle and warn or prevent the driver from making a dangerous mistake (Technology Review, 2017). ADAS systems used until now are specialized high-end hardware and are mainly available only for premium cars (e.g. Mercedes-Benz Attention Assists), but also a couple of smartphone based ADAS systems started to appear. Some of the applications on the market are used to monitor the road to avoid obstacles, vehicle headway, lane departure or speed infractions (iOnRoad official website, 2017, Augmented Driving website, 2017, Driver Guard application, 2017, Drive Safe application, 2017), or both safety and eco-driving (Birrell & Fowkes, 2014). Among other applications that can be mentioned is the CarSafe app which is used to detect driver drowsiness or distractions (You et al., 2013). One of the most advanced driving monitoring smartphone application is DriveSafe (Bergasa, Almería, Almazán, Yebes, & Arroyo, 2014). The application can detect inattentive driving behaviors, drowsiness and distracted drivers by using the rear-camera, the microphone, the inertial sensors and the GPS. Similar applications were also reported in various research studies (Chang et al., 2014, Chuang et al., 2014, Fang et al., 2014, Kasneci et al., 2017, Li et al., 2016, Meseguer et al., 2013, Musicant and Lotan, 2016, Qiao et al., 2016, Wang et al., 2016, Xiao and Feng, 2016).

Although the benefits of smartphone based ADAS systems on improving the driving performance and increasing safety are recognized there are only few recent studies that investigate the influence of using these assistive systems for driving performance. ADAS smartphone applications are able to estimate if the driver's behavior is safe or not (Eren, Makinist, Akin, & Yilmaz, 2012). The driver's degree of fatigue can be estimated by carefully monitoring his/her facial features, especially by analyzing the eye blinking frequency (Li et al., 2016, Qiao et al., 2016). By using a dual camera smartphone, both the driver's gaze and the moving obstacles situated in front of the car can be monitored (Xiao & Feng, 2016). Smartphone based ADAS can be used to detect distracted drivers while taking selfie photos and drawing back their attention to the road (Girbacia et al., 2016). These smartphone applications can be very useful in developing a cautious driving style, especially for young drivers (Musicant et al., 2016). A study in which the drivers used an ADAS for two weeks, revealed that the participants began to decelerate when receiving imminent collision warning (Botzer, Musicant, & Perry, 2017).

The research question addressed in this paper asks whether the use of an ADAS can counteract the distraction effects of addictive social network factors while driving. To achieve this, it was evaluated how a smartphone based driver assistance system influences the driver's behavior in various distraction scenarios. Parameters like number of lane departures, number of space cushion, average speed or number of speed infractions were analyzed in various scenarios to validate the proposed ADAS. The presented ADAS solution is a simple and relatively cheap system to implement, ensuring the active safety within the vehicle and traffic. The simple usage is backed up by the fact that the application can be used on any type of smartphone running on Android OS, the most spread operating system within mobile communication. Also, the vehicle type does not condition its use.

The most commonly downloaded applications on Google Play and App Store (iPhone) are applications for social networking, instant messaging and gaming. The most downloaded application in the UK, France, Germany, Poland and Australia, on Google Play was Facebook (Ofcom, 2016). In 2014, most users of social platforms were Facebook users (1.184 billions), followed by QQ users (816 millions) (Weare Social, 2014). In a report regarding the age of Facebook users, the largest percentage was the interval between 18 and 29 years (87% of adults) (Sprout social, 2015). Based on these statistical reports, in the present study was used the Facebook application as a distraction factor.
